The commit adds `incremental.py` only (viewed against `synccli.py` from the initial commit). Real defects:

**1. Major — `filter_excluded` mutable default argument (`excluded=[]`)**
The docstring promises "the subset of paths that must be excluded", but when called without `excluded`, the same list object is reused across calls and results accumulate: a second call returns all previously excluded paths plus new ones. Callers relying on the return value will wrongly skip files on every sync after the first — files silently never reach the destination. (It also mutates any list the caller passes in, an undocumented side effect.)

**2. Major — `copy_many` reports failed copies as done**
`copy_file` (synccli.py:62) already swallows `OSError` internally and returns normally, so a copy that failed to open/read/write still reaches `done.append(dst)`; the `try/except Exception: continue` only catches non-OSError errors. The returned "dst paths done" therefore includes files that were never actually copied — false success reporting that can lead a caller to record unsynced files as current (silent data loss / stale destination treated as up to date).

**3. Minor — `merge_manifest` never persists anything**
The name and docstring ("Merge new file states into the destination manifest") imply the file at `dst_manifest_path` is updated, but the function only reads it and returns the merged dict. A caller believing the contract will never write the result, leaving the on-disk manifest stale. Related: entries for files deleted on the source side are kept forever, so the merged manifest drifts from reality.

**4. Minor — `merge_manifest` crashes on malformed manifests**
`old[rel]["mtime"]` assumes every existing entry has an `mtime` key; a truncated/corrupt or older-format manifest raises a bare `KeyError`, aborting the merge (and, combined with defect 3, leaving no manifest written at all).
